{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | sed -n '1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Call-site provenance: occurrence contract and durable correspondence\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: architect design; no implementation or adoption acceptance claimed.\n     4\tAuthority: signed Addendum A v3 and signed attribution ruling v4.\n     5\tScope: read-only source investigation; this findings document is the sole output.\n     6\tDesign revision: 1 (2026-11-06).\n     7\t\n     8\tCitation convention: paths beginning `yang/` or `yin/` are under `src/cljc/`; a shortened filename inherits the nearest explicit namespace/path. `ruling-of-record.md` abbreviates the signed 1794600000000 ruling named in §1; `code-as-tuples.md` abbreviates `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`. Line ranges identify inspected source, while normative decisions in C1/D1 and the acceptance gates are new design requirements.\n     9\t\n    10\t## 1. Decision and evidence limits\n    11\t\n    12\tAdopt one language-neutral application-occurrence contract over the existing occurrence relation, one reserved frontend-metadata key, and the already-ruled third trace field. Keep code content, provenance, attribution evidence, and verification results separate. Publish the durable correspondence as an additional addressable record referring to the oracle's evidence **without changing that evidence's format**. Production adoption includes Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, Python, their preludes, and the additional ingress paths inventoried below.\n    13\t\n    14\tThe authority is `collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-RECORD.md:13-29` (A1–A4 and measurements), and `collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md:11-73` (R1–R7). In particular, signed A4 at line 25 explicitly strikes “or atomically with”: **implementation AND independent reconstruction must pass BEFORE acceptance of the first additional relying consumer**. Simultaneous code landing is possible; simultaneous acceptance before reconstruction is not. The oracle remains unblocked.\n    15\t\n    16\tRead order was addendum, architectural foundations, signed base ruling, then the recorded Astra addendum review. Astra's final review is the JSON agent-message at `1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log:48`; signed v3 supersedes its earlier “before, or atomically with” wording. No separate qwen addendum review was present in `collab/yang-clojure/` during this inspection. The available `1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md` is a base-ruling review, not the missing addendum review. Signed v3 records incorporation of qwen's amendments at lines 3–4.\n    17\t\n    18\tSource inspection occurred in a live workspace: master initially reported `e158afca`, later `5854869c5117e3659d8ce3bc3f140d017d7b8687`. Citations below identify inspected file locations, not a claim that all work was read from an immutable commit. Rebase/inventory refresh after the cell-ref sweeps and J2 is an implementation prerequisite. No tests, compilers, generators, or publication commands were run for this design.\n    19\t\n    20\t### 1.1 The oracle format is not yet available to freeze\n    21\t\n    22\tThe inspected worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-aaq`, branch `attribution-as-query`, reports HEAD `3eebe844e406ba56bc38a5f4b4a0098d811b5efd`. Its tracked `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1794-1839` still generates two-field body-entry traces and uses the offset accounting at lines 1786–1819. Its `BRIEF-aaq.md:15-21` requires the future inventory, three-field instrumentation, and versioned mechanically derived site tables; it does not define their schema. Untracked `probe/aaq_probe.clj:17-29` uses exploratory map-key paths, not the canonical row-position paths; it is not a published contract. The local dispatch log `collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log:1` reports an unrecognized-model error. This does not establish the status of work elsewhere.\n    23\t\n    24\tConsequently **the oracle evidence schema's exact identifier, field names, and canonical-source-hash algorithm/profile are unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides from the oracle's accepted record**. This document specifies the envelope around that record and its verification obligations, not an independently invented replacement site-table schema. “OracleEvidence” below means the entire accepted versioned oracle value, verbatim, including its site ids, argument-shape declarations, fixture linkage, trace schema, and version. Before durable implementation is accepted, the inventory must pin its concrete file, commit, schema identifier/version, validator, and derivation entry point. Absence of that pin blocks durable acceptance; it does not block the oracle round.\n    25\t\n    26\tThis is the format-race control demanded by A4. An implementation must not adopt a hypothetical table in this document or mistake `throw_sites.edn` for the new caller-site table.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. Occurrence contract C1\n    29\t\n    30\tThe following is the proposed normative contract, grounded in `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:393-455,658-665,918-931`.\n    31\t\n    32\t### 2.1 Identity and scope\n    33\t\n    34\tAn application site is `K = [origin root-address path]`.\n    35\t\n    36\t- `root-address` is the canonical root of the particular final UAST tree being named, not the application's subtree address or an instrumented runtime-map hash.\n    37\t- `path` is the grammar's structural path: scalar child slot steps are row positions; a child in a `nodes` slot is `[slot-position member-index]`. The root is `[]`. Never substitute map keys, host iteration order, allocation ids, source line numbers, PCs, callee names, or arities.\n    38\t- `origin` identifies the actual admission or generation event. Admitted syntax uses `[:source medium batch-token j]`; generated syntax uses `[:expansion expansion-record-address]`. Generated post-expansion trees must not be mislabeled as original source or given a fabricated expansion address. If a no-op expansion retains an admitted tree, the original source origin remains valid; “post-expansion” names the pipeline boundary, not proof that a generating event occurred.\n    39\t- An origin is supplied by the admitting/generating composition. Two separately admitted identical sources have different origins; two identical applications in one tree have different paths; two expansion attempts with identical output have different producing records. Replaying a single recorded admission is different from admitting the same text again.\n    40\t\n    41\tThe implemented origin validator accepts exactly the source and expansion shapes (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:938-958`). `vm/occurrences` returns `[root path node-id]`, one per place, with origin fixed by the composition; it does not return full keys or count only distinct row ids (`vm.cljc:1589-1609`).\n    42\t\n    43\tCoverage is **every final `:application` occurrence**, including generated wrappers, adapters, dispatcher/helper calls, bundled preludes, imported preludes, recursion, and statically indirect applications. “Source call” and “attributable upstream call” are projections over this coverage, not filters that delete other occurrences. Rule R's `yin/def` is structurally an application but lowers to `:define`; it gets an occurrence annotation and never a fictitious callee-entry event (`semantic/linearize.cljc:106-116`).\n    44\t\n    45\tDedicated operations such as `:dao.stream.apply/call`, stream nodes, and continuation nodes retain existing instruction provenance. They are not silently counted as `:application` sites. Their adapters' application occurrences are covered; an extension to count other node kinds must explicitly version the coverage policy (`vm.cljc:1015-1028`; `semantic/linearize.cljc:126-139`).\n    46\t\n    47\t### 2.2 Minimal persisted annotation\n    48\t\n    49\tReserve `:yang/call-site` in the existing frontend-metadata relation:\n    50\t\n    51\t```clojure\n    52\t[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]\n    53\t```\n    54\t\n    55\tThe integer `1` versions this key's coverage/interpretation contract. The first three coordinates ARE the site id; the value does not repeat it. This key is distinct from existing `:yang/site`, whose meaning is safepoint marks (`src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:55-75`). It carries no language name, source arity, ambient-context classification, or callee identity. Those are consumer evidence derived by the oracle or another explicitly versioned consumer.\n    56\t\n    57\tThe marker certifies that a producer preserved this occurrence under C1; the application predicate itself remains derived from canonical rows. There is no new canonical fact asserting what the grammar already says. A consumer can derive the eligible application set and compare it with the complete marker set. Under a C1 publication claim the sets must be equal; mere absence of annotations in an older publication is not false evidence of zero applications.\n    58\t\n    59\tEmit precisely one logical marker per eligible occurrence. Duplicate identical transport deliveries may be deduplicated as the same immutable fact; duplicate entries in a declared canonical table, conflicting values, unsupported versions, nonexistent paths, non-application targets, and missing/extra rows under a complete-coverage claim are validation failures. Other frontend metadata is preserved independently.\n    60\t\n    61\tImplementation may add this marker to temporary frontend map nodes before side-table projection, since the current extractor recognizes namespace `yang` (`vm.cljc:1183-1214`). Prefer deriving the relation directly from validated rows where map attachment would be redundant. `ast->semantic-bytecode` hashes only grammar slots and drops other node keys (`vm.cljc:1113-1123`); no grammar slot, instruction operand, canonical datom slot, or AST contract revision is authorized by C1.\n    62\t\n    63\t### 2.3 Finalization and transformations\n    64\t\n    65\tFinalize paths only after macro expansion, generated-name canonicalization, wrappers, module/SCC assembly, and tail marking that determine the named tree. Source-level annotations alone cannot cover generated calls (base ruling R2 at line 17). Low-level functions returning fragments may defer finalization until an owning root and origin exist; each executable/publication ingress must invoke the common finalizer. A frontend cannot claim adoption by marking just its parser or user-call lowering arm.\n    66\t\n    67\tA subsequent tree-changing transform creates another root and another occurrence relation. It must preserve an explicit old/new occurrence correspondence where it makes an attribution claim, recompute C1 for new applications, and identify generated helper calls separately. It must not retain stale paths or equate every new helper with the nearest source call. If a transform deletes, duplicates, or merges occurrences, its correspondence must record the actual multiplicity; ambiguity refuses attribution.\n    68\t\n    69\tMacro expansion already records input root, call path, macro root, parent, output and attempt (`src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1000,1062-1119`). However, final tail marking and stand-in lowering happen later (`:1255-1260`). The implementation inventory must resolve how final published roots are related to these expansion outputs. Whether the existing event/record adapters already provide the complete final-root correspondence is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. Do not assume an intermediate output record identifies every occurrence in the final assembled tree, and do not mint a fake `:expand` merely to satisfy the shape.\n    70\t\n    71\t### 2.4 Lowering, reconstruction, and runtime lookup\n    72\t\n    73\tPersist the existing instruction relation `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`. The row linearizer already returns `{:address ... :provenance [[pc origin root path] ...]}`; its caller supplies the segment coordinate (`semantic/linearize.cljc:188-214`; code-as-tuples §5.3 at lines 918–931). A call dispatch looks up its **own call instruction's** emitting occurrence, not the preceding operand's instruction or the callee's body. Multiple instructions may belong to one occurrence.\n    74\t\n    75\tSegment+PC alone is insufficient when identical code is loaded under two origins. The runtime must retain its load/admission origin alongside its image reference when selecting provenance. This is an occurrence-scoped runtime context, not a new code hash. The native image derivations must supply or independently reconstruct a mapping from their call PCs back through semantic PCs to K; PC equality across formats must not be assumed. Current semantic `resolve` preserves PCs, register derivation preserves layout, and stack derivation emits a new sequence (`semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-93,154-172`).\n    76\t\n    77\tCanonical reconstruction deliberately shares nodes by content (`vm.cljc:1477-1486,1522-1525`). Never cache an occurrence annotation by node-id or mutate a shared canonical reconstruction and expect distinct sites to survive. Keep an occurrence cursor/side lookup, or expand occurrence-specific ephemeral images. The datom linearizer currently returns `[pc entity]`, not K (`semantic/linearize.cljc:283-302`); use an explicit entity-to-occurrence bridge with the owning batch, root and path, refusing ambiguity. `encoder/datom-entity-occurrences` and the envelope's `:yin/entity-occurrences` are the starting machinery (`encoder.cljc:67,115-148`).\n    78\t\n    79\t### 2.5 Ephemeral origin and trace field\n    80\t\n    81\tName the nonpersistent domain **ephemeral instrumentation origin**. Its C1 spelling is:\n    82\t\n    83\t```clojure\n    84\t[:ephemeral evidence-scope-token compilation-token member-index]\n    85\t;; ephemeral site:\n    86\t[ephemeral-origin canonical-compiled-root structural-path]\n    87\t;; already signed trace shape:\n    88\t[fname arity caller-site-id]\n    89\t```\n    90\t\n    91\tThe composition supplies two non-nil plain-data tokens: `evidence-scope-token` names the recording/fixture scope; `compilation-token` names this particular instrumented compilation in that scope. `member-index` is a nonnegative integer. They are explicitly recorded inputs, not inferred from source hash, host object identity, time, lane name, or callee. Both oracle C lanes execute the SAME compiled artifact with the same tokens; a separate compilation gets another token. Replay uses the recorded tokens. A source hash alone would collapse distinct attempts.\n    92\t\n    93\tThis is a precise proposed spelling for the still-unfrozen oracle implementation. It is **not** a third legal persisted occurrence origin: `vm/occurrence-origin?` must continue rejecting it. An EDN evidence file may preserve an ephemeral id as evidence of that run; that does not convert it into a portable code occurrence. Persisted C1 side tables and expansion links require real ruled origins.\n    94\t\n    95\tThe canonical compiled root is computed after code-producing instrumentation that changes the AST, before occurrence stamps are associated into runtime maps. Runtime stamping must not introduce a root-hash self-reference. If instrumentation subsequently changes structure, it must carry an explicit transform mapping or recompute the frozen site table; no path can silently retain its old meaning.\n    96\t\n    97\tThe oracle must finalize the spelling in its own versioned evidence contract and use that format once. If its accepted schema differs by the time implementation starts, preserve its evidence verbatim and publish a reviewed binding of its origin fields to this semantic domain; do not rewrite its tables behind an unchanged version. A semantic conflict (no compilation scope, map-key paths, callee ids) requires correction on the oracle's record, not a silent normalization in durable verification.\n    98\t\n    99\tRuntime `assoc` stamping is explicitly licensed by §2.6, owner ruling 2026-09-15: the walker holds an ephemeral image and nothing runtime-associated is projected back to canonical rows; runtime keys must be stripped at serialization/projection of continuation frames (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:459-474`). Static frontend marker extraction is a separate, pre-execution side-table operation. This clause licenses oracle-specific walker instrumentation; it does not establish ordinary production coverage.\n   100\t\n   101\tCapture the caller K at actual application dispatch, after operator/operand evaluation, and preserve it until that callee's body-entry record. Nested argument calls must not overwrite it. Tail dispatch carries the new caller K even if it reuses a frame. A helper's call carries the helper's own K; no automatic inheritance of upstream credit. Unknown caller linkage yields no credit. Repeated recursion can record the same site many times; R3's consumer is existence-based, not a proof of invocation-by-invocation causality (`ruling-of-record.md:17-34`).\n   102\t\n   103\t## 3. A2 inventory: revisions, preservation, and refusal\n   104\t\n   105\tThis is a design inventory of the reachable surfaces found in the inspected sources. The implementation's first deliverable refreshes file/symbol locations after the prerequisite merges, expands each wildcard artifact family into an exact list, and assigns every item “changed”, “read/verified”, or “not reached with proof”. Newly discovered producers cannot be excluded merely because this table predates their merge.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 3.1 Contract revision decisions\n   108\t\n   109\t| Contract | Decision for this design | Revision trigger and mismatch behavior |\n   110\t|---|---|---|\n   111\t| C1 frontend metadata | New reserved key/value version 1 and coverage validator. Existing five-coordinate relation is unchanged. | Unknown marker version or malformed/incomplete claimed annotation refuses C1-dependent attribution. Ordinary legacy code loading remains available. |\n   112\t| Oracle evidence | The oracle owns the new versioned site-table and three-field trace contract. Durable work imports it verbatim. | Two-field evidence is not silently upgraded to three fields. Wrong schema/hash/fixture/lane/site-shape refuses attribution. Preserve old evidence as historical data. |\n   113\t| Durable correspondence | New separate schema 1 below. | Unknown schema, missing evidence, failed reconstruction or ambiguous linkage refuses reliance, independently of code loadability. |\n   114\t| UAST/row/datom grammar | Read and validate; no C1 change. Current AST stamp is `v3`. | Existing `check-contract!` and structural/hash checks remain. Do not add a canonical slot or broaden origins to accept ephemeral ids. |\n   115\t| Semantic/stack/register execution contracts | Read and validate; no instruction-grammar change specified. Current stamps are `v4`, `b2`, `r2`. | Revise only if implementation changes instruction grammar or execution semantics. A new trace field in a harness stream alone does not revise these. |\n   116\t| Code lowering profiles | Read, and verify unchanged code output. A separately versioned provenance recipe names any new side mapping. | Revise the relevant code profile if a pinned lowering rule or emitted code changes, even if execution grammar does not. A side-only addition must not masquerade as validation by the old derivation verifier. |\n   117\t| Linker manifest | Keep schema 1 by publishing correspondence beside it. | Adding a manifest field requires a separately versioned manifest contract and validator; current closed keys reject it. No automatic schema-2 just because R1 is now active. |\n   118\t| Existing `:derive` record | Read/reference; do not add correspondence keys to it. | Current closed key set rejects extra fields and non-`:derive` ops. Reusing the record-format container for a richer correspondence would require a new/extended format, not an incidental extra field. |\n   119\t| Frontend catalog revision/profile | Change the frontend revision and relevant annotation/support profile when its publication/coverage promise changes. | Existing revision pinning and conflict/incompatible refusal remain; keep SPI 1 unless its actual request/response protocol changes. |\n   120\t| Safepoint, site-set and binder contracts | Read; preserve distinct purpose and identities. | Revise only if their serialized content/selection/transform rules change. Do not alias `:yang/call-site` to `:yang/site`. |\n   121\t| Continuation/UCF/checkpoint contracts | Read/inventory initially. | If a provenance-dependent consumer requires new serialized runtime state, inventory and version that actual state contract before shipping. A transient field cannot be silently serialized or relied on after migration. |\n   122\t| Parser/CST and `yin.cic.spec` | Read only for C1. | No parser grammar/CST revision or CIC v1 change follows merely from side metadata; R4 explicitly leaves CIC v1 untouched. |\n   123\t\n   124\tCurrent constants: `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:262-285`. Some design prose still says semantic `v3` (`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1879-1884`); implementation inventory must use the current source stamp, not copy stale prose. Revision principles: `code-as-tuples.md:859-913`, `yin.vm.linker.md:1871-1921`. Concrete closed validators: `linker.cljc:731-759,841-871`. Frontend revision semantics: `yang/frontend.cljc:15-16,87-98,166-200`.\n   125\t\n   126\tWhether some existing higher-level ledger/publication mechanism can carry this new correspondence without **its own** schema change is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. The narrower question is answered: the inspected linker's `record-defect` cannot accept extra keys under today's `:yin.ledger/record`. This design avoids making that compatibility claim.\n   127\t\n   128\t### 3.2 Producer-to-consumer preservation inventory\n   129\t\n   130\t| Surface and inspected locations | Required implementation work / read-only dependency |\n   131\t|---|---|\n   132\t| Clojure `compile-form`, `compile`, `compile-program`, `lower-unit`: `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2021,2164,2839,2993` | Cover native and argument-vector/outcome compilation, library/adapters, linked unit wrapping and global publication. Finalize at executable roots, after expansion. Existing fragment APIs need not invent origins. |\n   133\t| TypeScript packet/body/unit/multi-unit/stage: `yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:2262-2363` | Cover bundled and linked runtime paths, canonical names and tail marks, all units and generated runtime calls. Preserve packet/source association separately. |\n   134\t| PHP ANTLR body/program/packet/module/SCC/stage: `yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3737,3759,3800,3840,3855,3929,3998-4041` | Cover includes, class-map units, strict-types variants, SCC bundles/aliases, helpers and linked/bundled paths. |\n   135\t| Legacy PHP `compile-program/compile`: `yang/php.cljc:640-685` | Additional live frontend, not retired by the presence of ANTLR. Annotate all generated combinator/wrapper applications too. |\n   136\t| Python module/body/packet/SCC/stage: `yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:2018,2030,2097,2139,2198,2298,2334-2373`; session `repl.cljc:86-108` | Cover stateless and already-primed sessions, imports, bundles/aliases and generated body wrappers; first-session prelude coverage cannot stand in for subsequent submissions. |\n   137\t| REPL frontend catalog: `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:24-110` | Covers single-form vs multi-form Clojure, legacy PHP, Python session lowering and Jolt native parser selection. Standard catalog does not currently register TypeScript; direct TS paths still require adoption. |\n   138\t| Common UAST/prelude/call/module emitters: `yang/core/prelude.cljc:44-69,138-159`, `yang/core/call.cljc:184-191`, `yang/core/module.cljc:31-99` | These produce wrapper/helper/prelude code outside source-call arms. Apply common finalization at the owning publication, including direct hand-built UAST accepted as frontend output. |\n   139\t| Common streaming stage: `yang/core/stage.cljc:25-59` | It currently supplies medium plus packet unit as batch token. Audit distinct re-admissions of the same unit: C1 requires fresh admission identity while retry keeps the token; packet identity alone is insufficient if reused for distinct admissions. |\n   140\t| Encoder map/rows/datoms: `yin/vm/encoder.cljc:25-43,67-148,230-258` | Map projection emits side tables; already-projected rows currently return empty tables; envelope projection replaces table fields. Preserve/validate supplied metadata and finalize generated output instead of silently dropping it. Maintain batch member j. |\n   141\t| Macro pipeline: `yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1005,1062-1119,1196-1261` | Keep expansion attempt provenance, resolve final-root ownership, annotate generated occurrences and retain retrievable expansion records. Post-expansion is a required integration point. |\n   142\t| Safepoint insertion: `yang/safepoint.cljc:234-256` | Current derived envelope explicitly clears frontend metadata, source positions and entity occurrences. New root needs fresh C1 plus verified input/output mapping; preserve the safepoint record's separate semantics. |\n   143\t| Projection and row validation: `yin/vm.cljc:1113-1159,1217-1259,1262-1456,1589-1609` | Reuse grammar traversal, plain-data checks and occurrence enumeration. Add a side-table coverage validator, not C1 requirements to canonical row validation. Unannotated legacy rows remain valid. |\n   144\t| Canonical and datom reconstruction: `yin/vm.cljc:705,818-851,1477-1555` | Side metadata does not reappear from content alone. Attach provenance context out of band; do not collapse occurrence ids via shared reconstruction/entity ids. Round-trip canonical hashes must remain equal. |\n   145\t| Semantic row and datom lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc:106-128,188-221,283-302` | Persist returned provenance, supply origins explicitly, bridge datom entities with occurrences; nil-origin legacy calls remain loadable but cannot satisfy C1 attribution. |\n   146\t| Native lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-120,154-184` | Produce/reconstruct complete native-call-PC provenance; compose with semantic mapping. Sparse variable/closure diagnostics do not establish call coverage. Inventory any legacy direct de Bruijn lowering still reached after merges. |\n   147\t| Runtime dispatch and resume: `yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:273-332,493,625-711,776`; `yin/vm/semantic.cljc:102,142,333-380` | Cold/hot application routes, operator/operand frames, linked image attachment and resumption all need occurrence context for traced execution. Native engines and UCF lifting must be audited too; no claim they already preserve caller K. |\n   148\t| Existing derivation verification: `yin/vm/ledger.cljc:156-215`, `yin/vm/linker.cljc:2164-2255` | Existing verification re-lowers code and compares identities, not side metadata. Keep code verification and add independent provenance/correspondence verification. `:trusted` does not satisfy this new acceptance gate. |\n   149\t| Generic publication: `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:115-218` | Currently takes only `:vector` from `project-rows` at line 157. Publish complete side artifacts and origin/record closure beside code; do not discard provenance or change manifest keys casually. Validate before making a C1 availability claim. |\n   150\t| Unit publication: `yang/core/publication.cljc:92-170`, PHP `publication.cljc:27-70`, Python `publication.cljc:27-80` | Publish records for actual bundle roots; aliases cannot borrow unrelated paths. Coordinate side artifacts with index/discovery publication. PHP currently has nil site-set/binder hooks; Python's site-set hook is safepoint-specific. |\n   151\t| Site-set discovery/signing precedent: `yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:5-31,65-101` | Reuse the separate-record-by-manifest pattern and declared-principal verification where appropriate. Its exact key schema is not the caller-site or oracle schema. Inventory discovery/fetch/retention paths for the new record explicitly. |\n   152\t| Code index / republishing: `yin/repl/ast_index.cljc:3-24,108`; `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:225-300` | Query views can be rebuilt from rows but cannot recover omitted admission provenance. Session/index-to-module publication must preserve or explicitly remap occurrence roots, and retain original evidence sources. |\n   153\t| Oracle instrumentation/accounting: `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1786-1855,2341-2405,2432-2468` | Oracle-owned trace migration, offset retirement across the full family, both lanes, refusal pins, gate accounting and evidence regeneration per R5/R6. Audit table derivation independently per R7. |\n   154\t\n   155\t### 3.3 Persisted artifacts and publication blast radius\n   156\t\n   157\t“Side tables are additive” is the compatibility policy, not permission to skip republishing coverage evidence. **Unannotated persisted artifacts remain loadable under their supported existing code contracts.** A provenance-requiring query returns missing provenance/refuses credit; it never invents identity from names, hashes, arities, values or source lines. Old provenance may be reconstructed only when the actual admission/expansion inputs are retained, and the result is a newly verified artifact.\n   158\t\n   159\tThe implementation inventory must enumerate, regenerate where changed, and verify these families on **all four host lanes**:\n   160\t\n   161\t1. `yang.call`, Clojure `clj` and its library, TypeScript `js/jssp`, Python `py/pysp`, and PHP `php` plus its hook/runtime publications. Shared emitter anchors: `yang/core/call.cljc:184`, `yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:125-143`, TS `prelude.cljc:4168-4260`, Python `prelude.cljc:3645-3739`, PHP `prelude.cljc:5328-5411`. Include PHP's definition lists `prelude_module.cljc:386` and `prelude_p9.cljc:558`; their inclusion must not escape measurement because they are emitted through the main prelude.\n   162\t2. For each host publication: canonical row closure, semantic vector, stack/register stored images and checksums, derivation records, schema-1 manifest, pinned dependency addresses, new C1 side tables/provenance/correspondence, and discovery/index/name/signature records where used. The actual publication sequence is `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:156-209`. Side-only annotation should leave old code addresses unchanged; measure this rather than assume it. New side objects still need publication, retrieval and retention.\n   163\t3. Fresh process-local prelude caches, linked harnesses and golden addresses: TS `test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:55-78`, Python `test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:55-77`, PHP `test/yang/php/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:38-56`. Reusing a realized pre-change delay is not a republication test.\n   164\t4. Imported user modules, SCC bundles and aliases, class maps, safepointed derivatives, session/module exports, signed discovery entries and DHT fixtures. Specifically `test/resources/yang/python/dht-import-v1.edn` and its generator `dht-import-v1.generate.clj:1-20`, which says to regenerate whenever lowering/publication/prelude changes addresses. Preserve old fixtures as compatibility inputs where practical, rather than rewriting away the only legacy-load test.\n   165\t5. Oracle evidence: new versioned caller-site tables; `slice9_evidence.edn`, `slice9_verification.edn`, `slice9_declarations.edn`, `slice9_unexercised.edn`, `fixture_closure.edn`, gate markdown and captured verification/test outputs; `context.edn`, `kernel_gate.edn`, `manifest.edn` and earlier slice evidence as input/history or regeneration targets according to actual dependency. Every changed trace-bearing field/pin must be listed; blanket rewriting of historical evidence is not required.\n   166\t6. Explicitly inventory `test/yang/latte_oracle/throw_sites.edn:1-7` and `case_arms.edn:1-11`. They currently contain bare vectors of source inventory maps, with no top-level schema stamp. Their producer is `src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj:15-24,52-64`; throw-site equality checking is `latte_oracle_test.clj:679-699`. They are **read/validated unchanged** if their meaning/shape remains unchanged. If caller-site coordinates or new fields are added, version their evidence contract and update producer, validator, readers, generators and pins together. They are not automatically the R1 versioned application-site table, and source `:line` is not K.\n   167\t\n   168\tThe addendum names TypeScript `publication.cljc`, but no `src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/publication.cljc` exists in the inspected checkout. TS prelude publication is presently visible in its linked harness above; its design describes later publication work (`docs/design/yang.typescript.md:1347,1545`). Whether an in-flight seat supplies a new file, renamed path, or equivalent hook is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. It remains a named mandatory inventory item, not a silent omission or an invented present file.\n   169\t\n   170\t### 3.4 Failure separation\n   171\t\n   172\tPreserve the existing failure categories: malformed canonical content fails structural validation; wrong bytes/address fails hash verification; unknown execution contract refuses load; differing lowering profile reports profile mismatch or `:unverified-derivation`; wrong recomputed output reports derivation mismatch (`vm.cljc:283-285`; `ledger.cljc:156-180`; `linker.cljc:2175-2215`). New evidence-specific failures must be distinguishable: unsupported C1/evidence version, missing provenance, bad occurrence, incomplete coverage, wrong source/adaptation hash, missing lane, ambiguous correspondence, stale declared site, or reconstruction mismatch.\n   173\t\n   174\tA corrupted required provenance artifact refuses the provenance-dependent operation even if code itself verifies. An absent optional annotation does not make old code unloadable. A signature authenticates a publisher; it does not verify a correspondence. A trusted code link is not evidence of a recomputed correspondence.\n   175\t\n   176\t## 4. R1 durable correspondence schema D1\n   177\t\n   178\t### 4.1 Additive envelope\n   179\t\n   180\tDefine a new separately addressed plain-data object. The following field names and types specify D1; angle-bracket placeholders are metavariables, not values to persist.\n   181\t\n   182\t```clojure\n   183\t{:yang.correspondence/schema 1\n   184\t :yang.correspondence/evidence <Jing address of OracleEvidence>\n   185\t :yang.correspondence/recipe <Jing address of reconstruction recipe>\n   186\t :yang.correspondence/inputs [<Jing address> ...]\n   187\t :yang.correspondence/roots [<canonical UAST root address> ...]\n   188\t :yang.correspondence/origin-bindings\n   189\t [[<ephemeral site id from OracleEvidence> <persisted occurrence key>] ...]\n   190\t :yang.correspondence/artifacts\n   191\t [{:format <existing format keyword>\n   192\t   :identity <that format's identity>\n   193\t   :storage <Jing address of retrievable artifact>\n   194\t   :derivation <Jing address of applicable derivation record>} ...]\n   195\t :yang.correspondence/metadata <Jing address of C1 frontend-metadata rows>\n   196\t :yang.correspondence/instruction-provenance\n   197\t [<Jing address of an instruction-provenance relation> ...]}\n   198\t```\n   199\t\n   200\tThe D1 key set is closed; vectors preserve declared order and contain no duplicate declarations. Empty artifact/provenance vectors are allowed for a tree-only consumer, whose claimed coverage must say tree-only. `artifacts` lists lowered images only; canonical roots are retrievable row closures listed separately. Native image identity need not equal its storage address: the publisher already distinguishes checksums and stored images (`publish.cljc:159-185,204-208`). The record's address is the ordinary Jing content address of the whole map; no self-address field.\n   201\t\n   202\t`evidence` resolves to **OracleEvidence verbatim**: same EDN data shape, schema/version, field names, vectors, site ids, source-hash value and classifications. Do not project a subset or copy its rows into a second durable site-table shape. Store the whole value through a lossless codec and prove round-trip equality; preserve the original checked-in EDN bytes separately as a source artifact when byte-level archival is needed. Jing storage addressing does not replace the oracle's own canonical-source-hash algorithm. New adoption evidence must use the oracle format as frozen; unsupported representational needs require an explicit versioned extension owned by that format, never an ad hoc competing table.\n   203\t\n   204\t`inputs` names the retrievable source/adaptation/fixture/profile inputs required by the recipe, including the inlined adapter and declarations that the oracle actually hashed. A repository commit string alone is not a fetchable reconstruction input. `roots` names the produced code trees. Expansion records and admitted source-envelope identities required by the bindings must be in the retrievable input closure. The input/source artifacts may be data blobs; they must not be represented as UAST addresses unless they actually are UAST.\n   205\t\n   206\tThe recipe is a closed versioned data description, proposed as:\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t{:yang.correspondence.recipe/schema 1\n   210\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/function <qualified interpreter identifier>\n   211\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/profile <address of exact reconstruction profile>\n   212\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/input-roles [[<role keyword> <input index>] ...]\n   213\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/evidence-contract <oracle's exact schema identity>\n   214\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/coverage <address of declared coverage policy>}\n   215\t```\n   216\t\n   217\tThe recipe profile pins frontend/compiler revision, grammar/options, expansion environment and recorded origins/attempts, all code-producing transformations, source canonicalization, site-table derivation, and lowering/provenance rules. The consumer must implement that exact recipe/profile. A missing upstream canonicalization rule is not repaired by choosing a plausible hash. The exact oracle schema identity remains the dependency in §1.1.\n   218\t\n   219\t`origin-bindings` is an added relation binding evidence-local sites to actual durable occurrences; **it is not a replacement attribution table**. It preserves the oracle's ephemeral ids literally. Validate every endpoint, reconstructed tree and path. A site's mapping must be unique within the claimed compilation. Multiple replay records can name the same durable occurrence only where they explicitly replay the same admission; distinct admissions must remain distinct. An oracle run without durable expansion/source records cannot be promoted merely by changing `:ephemeral` to `:expansion`. It may remain useful oracle evidence without a durable binding. To accept a relying consumer, reproduce the actual compilation under recorded durable admissions/expansions and verify its correspondence; do not retroactively assert an unrecorded event.\n   220\t\n   221\t### 4.2 Independent reconstruction and discovery\n   222\t\n   223\tThe D1 producer is the compilation/publication composition that can retain inputs, origins and outputs. The canonical compiler and lowerer remain oblivious to attribution policy except for the side artifacts they emit. D1 is an ordinary record on a separate publication/ledger stream, discoverable by its address and its code/evidence associations. The Python site-set record is a concrete precedent for “record names manifest, manifest keeps schema” (`siteset.cljc:5-22,79-101`). The implementation must pin the actual discovery schema, signature/index projection and retention owner before acceptance; this document does not pretend the current linker automatically fetches D1.\n   224\t\n   225\tVerification proceeds in this order:\n   226\t\n   227\t1. Fetch D1, recipe, OracleEvidence and every required source/row/provenance artifact; verify their addresses and structural schemas. Reject an unsupported oracle schema before interpreting any row.\n   228\t2. Verify each code image under its own execution contract and its existing derivation chain. Report content integrity separately from code derivation.\n   229\t3. Reconstruct the source/adaptation compilation under the exact recipe, using recorded source admissions and expansion attempts; recompute final roots and eligible occurrence sets. Recompute the oracle's canonical-source-hash using its exact rule.\n   230\t4. Independently re-derive the oracle site declarations from compiled AST argument expressions. Compare with OracleEvidence verbatim under that schema's comparison rules. Do not make the producer's serialized classifications the verifier's input oracle.\n   231\t5. Reconstruct every claimed ephemeral-to-persisted binding, C1 marker set and instruction mapping. Verify exact coverage and all multiplicities, including helpers that receive no upstream credit.\n   232\t6. Execute the attribution query over separately scoped evidence/occurrence/row relations; require actual body entry in both required oracle C lanes. Reconstruct the credit set and report withdrawn witnesses and stale declarations. R7 requires re-derivation, not just re-running a query over trusted declarations (`ruling-of-record.md:48-50`).\n   233\t7. Publish a separate verification result naming D1's address, recipe/profile, verifier revision, inputs, outcomes and evidence. D1 cannot mark itself “verified”. The first additional consumer's acceptance must cite this completed result.\n   234\t\n   235\tAn implementation may share the schema decoder and canonical primitives, but a verifier must not call the producer's table derivation as its only “independent” check. The existing table generator resolves the oracle's own private derivation vars (`latte_oracle_tables.clj:15-18`); regeneration through it alone does not satisfy R7.\n   236\t\n   237\tD1 does not revise old site tables, force old module manifests to schema 2, or automatically validate the oracle's historical evidence. If actual integration requires changing a closed publication record, that specific schema revision returns to the A2 inventory. Unknown recipes refuse verification; `:trusted` is never substituted for `:verifying` (linker design `:1902-1921`).\n   238\t\n   239\t## 5. A3 adoption briefs and ordering\n   240\t\n   241\t**Scheduling:** frontend adoption starts after the in-flight cell-ref sweeps and the J2 re-run land. Refresh this inventory on that merged base before changing shared prelude/publication paths. The current sweeps, J2 and oracle continue under their existing scopes. This design does not merge, pause, or extend their tasks (signed A3/A4 at lines 21–25).\n   242\t\n   243\tEach adoption brief must cite this findings file and C1/D1 by revision. The shared occurrence finalizer/validator and provenance publication work precede or accompany frontend implementation; D1 independent verification precedes acceptance of the first frontend consumer that relies on D1. A frontend may adopt static annotation without consuming oracle attribution, but cannot call that durable correspondence acceptance.\n   244\t\n   245\t### 5.1 Mandatory host sets\n   246\t\n   247\tThese are acceptance requirements, not claims that current tests already pass. **No frontend may drop Jolt because shared code compiled on another host.** Host lanes and VM execution formats are separate axes.\n   248\t\n   249\t| Frontend | JVM | Node | Dart | Jolt |\n   250\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   251\t| Production Clojure | Reader/source, all production entry points, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same portable source/forms and paths, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same | Same; run current reader limitations as explicit refusals, not substitute parsers |\n   252\t| TypeScript / JS dialect | Local source parser plus portable CST lowering, bundled/linked/prelude publications and load/query | Same pinned source-derived CST through portable lowering; parserless import/load; fresh prelude publication | Same packet/import/publication gate | Same packet/import/publication gate; Jolt load/parity is required |\n   253\t| PHP ANTLR + legacy PHP | Local ANTLR source parser, portable packet paths, includes/class-map/SCC, both compiler families and publications | Same source-derived CST for ANTLR; legacy source compiler; fresh publications and imports | Same | Same packet/legacy source/publication/load gate |\n   254\t| Python | Local source parser, packet/module/import/SCC/session paths, publications | Local generated parser plus all packet/session/publication paths | Local generated parser plus all packet/session/publication paths | Native C++ Python parser plus packet/session/publication/load paths; unavailable parser is a failed required source gate with a recorded reason, not a green skip |\n   255\t\n   256\tTS local parser restriction is recorded at `docs/design/yang.typescript.md:113-116,517-518`; its packet-level execution policy at `:1325-1326`. Python's host selection is implemented in `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:71-110`; Jolt's native Python adapter describes its actual parser and refusal in `src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:1-28`. PHP's local ANTLR parser is `src/clj/yang/php/antlr/cst.clj`, while the independent portable legacy compiler remains registered at `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:69-70`. Existing Jolt applicability exclusions are explicit per-test data (`test/resources/yin/repl/jolt-applicability.edn:1-5,46-48`); they cannot be expanded to evade these new gates.\n   257\t\n   258\tOn each host run static occurrence/provenance checks for AST, semantic, stack and register artifacts where the existing format admits the fixture. Execute direct walker and all three vector paths for the portable correctness corpus. Linked paths exercise all supported linked formats and pin existing unsupported/refusal cases explicitly. For example, the inspected TS linked harness documents a walker `:undeclared-free` limitation while using three vector VMs (`test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:11-15`); this design does not silently promise that old unrelated limitation is fixed. If a missing lane prevents the required new provenance test, report adoption incomplete with the named dependency.\n   259\t\n   260\t### 5.2 Cross-host equality without collapsing admissions\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short; git rev-parse HEAD; rg -n 'slice9_site_tables|slice9_correspondence|OracleEvidence|site-tables' src test collab/yang-clojure docs\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
