                    (and (= 5 (count row)) (= call-site-key (nth row 3))))\\n                       (distinct frontend-metadata))\\n@@ -188,7 +201,13 @@\\n         (throw (ex-info \\\"Unsupported call-site marker version\\\"\\n                         {:rule :yang/call-site-unsupported-version,\\n                          :site [o r path], :value value})))\\n-      (let [tag (when (and (= o origin) (= r root)) (get tag-at [r path]))]\\n+      (when (and per-occurrence-origins? (not (vm/occurrence-origin? o)))\\n+        (throw (ex-info \\\"Call-site marker carries a malformed occurrence origin\\\"\\n+                        {:rule :yang/call-site-invalid-origin,\\n+                         :site [o r path], :origin o})))\\n+      (let [tag (when (and (= r root)\\n+                           (or per-occurrence-origins? (= o origin)))\\n+                  (get tag-at [r path]))]\\n         (cond\\n           (nil? tag)\\n           (throw (ex-info \\\"Call-site marker names no occurrence\\\"\\n@@ -198,7 +217,11 @@\\n           (throw (ex-info \\\"Call-site marker on a non-application occurrence\\\"\\n                           {:rule :yang/call-site-non-application,\\n                            :site [o r path], :tag tag})))))\\n-    (let [marked (marked-application-sites frontend-metadata)]\\n+    (let [marked (if per-occurrence-origins?\\n+                   (into #{}\\n+                         (map (fn [[_ r path]] [r path]))\\n+                         (marked-application-sites frontend-metadata))\\n+                   (marked-application-sites frontend-metadata))]\\n       (when (not= eligible marked)\\n         (throw (ex-info \\\"Call-site coverage is not complete\\\"\\n                         {:rule :yang/call-site-incomplete-coverage,\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\\nindex 5cfd9f33..83cfdb33 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\\n@@ -101,24 +101,46 @@\\n ;; reference `{table record}` — the module closure follows manifest\\n ;; references, so only a referenced table travels with the module\\n ;; through transfer. The reverse record names the tree root, the\\n-;; publisher's origin, and the table's address; it cannot name the\\n-;; manifest (the manifest references it, so the two addresses would be\\n-;; circular), and custody runs one way, from the manifest forward. The\\n-;; publisher extracts the markers from the map AST, refuses the\\n+;; publication's recorded admission origin, and the table's address; it\\n+;; cannot name the manifest (the manifest references it, so the two\\n+;; addresses would be circular), and custody runs one way, from the\\n+;; manifest forward. The publication's origin is an ADMISSION identity\\n+;; (design §2.1): an explicit token drawn per publication — so two\\n+;; separately published identical trees are two admissions with\\n+;; distinct origins — persisted in the record and retrieved from the\\n+;; record, never reconstructed from the tree's content. The publisher\\n+;; extracts the markers from the map AST, refuses the\\n ;; publication when they do not exactly cover the canonical tree's\\n ;; application occurrences — or when a marker's value is not version 1 —\\n ;; and otherwise materializes the table and its record into the same\\n ;; handle. A module with no markers makes no claim and publishes exactly\\n ;; as before.\\n \\n+(def ^:private publication-sequence\\n+  \\\"The publisher's admission counter. Each publication is one admission\\n+   event (design §2.1): two separately admitted identical sources have\\n+   different origins, so the token drawn here never repeats.\\\"\\n+  (atom 0))\\n+\\n+(defn- next-admission-token\\n+  \\\"A fresh, never-repeated admission token for one publication.\\\"\\n+  []\\n+  (keyword \\\"yin.vm.linker.publish.publication\\\"\\n+           (str (swap! publication-sequence inc))))\\n+\\n (defn- publication-origin\\n-  \\\"The occurrence origin of a module tree this publisher admits: the\\n-   publisher's own medium, the tree's root as its batch token, member\\n-   0. Deterministic in the tree, so the table published beside a\\n-   manifest can be recomputed — and its absence detected — from the\\n-   manifest alone.\\\"\\n-  [root]\\n-  [:source :yin.vm.linker.publish/publish root 0])\\n+  \\\"The occurrence origin of the publication admission `token` names:\\n+   the publisher's own medium, the explicit admission token, member 0.\\n+   `token` is the publication's admission identity — the\\n+   `:yin.link.publish/admission` option when the composition pins one,\\n+   otherwise a publisher-allocated token that never repeats — so two\\n+   publications of identical trees are two admissions with distinct\\n+   origins. The origin is an admission event's identity, not a\\n+   function of the tree's content: it is persisted in the side record\\n+   at publication time and retrieved from that record; nothing ever\\n+   reconstructs it from the root.\\\"\\n+  [token]\\n+  [:source :yin.vm.linker.publish/publish token 0])\\n \\n \\n (defn- claims-call-sites?\\n@@ -137,9 +159,9 @@\\n    do not; `{:unsupported-version v :path p}` when a marker's value is\\n    not version 1 — a claim rejection, never a silent acceptance of a\\n    table a retriever would read as version 1.\\\"\\n-  [ast tree]\\n+  [ast tree admission]\\n   (when (claims-call-sites? ast)\\n-    (let [origin (publication-origin (:root tree))\\n+    (let [origin (publication-origin admission)\\n           extracted (filter #(= :yang/call-site (nth % 3))\\n                             (:frontend-metadata\\n                               (vm/ast-side-tables ast tree origin)))\\n@@ -212,11 +234,17 @@\\n              (footprint handle (assoc spec :tree tree)))\\n         ;; a C1 claim is validated before anything is written: a module\\n         ;; whose markers do not cover its tree is never published as\\n-        ;; though it did\\n+        ;; though it did. One publication is one admission event\\n+        ;; (design §2.1): the admission identity is pinned explicitly by\\n+        ;; the composition when it supplies `:yin.link.publish/admission`,\\n+        ;; otherwise drawn fresh here — either way it is persisted in\\n+        ;; the side record and retrieved from there\\n+        admission (or (:yin.link.publish/admission opts)\\n+                      (next-admission-token))\\n         claim (when (and tree (not defect) (not requirement)\\n                          (not= :refused (:status tree))\\n                          (not= :refused (:status fp)))\\n-                (call-site-claim ast tree))]\\n+                (call-site-claim ast tree admission))]\\n     (cond\\n       undefined\\n       (refusal :yin.link.publish/undefined-export {:export undefined})\\n@@ -362,11 +390,13 @@\\n    when the module made no C1 claim. The manifest references the table\\n    and its reverse record (`:yin.module/call-sites`), so the reference —\\n    not a recomputation — is what is resolved here: the rows are fetched\\n-   from the referenced table address and held to the record the\\n-   publication wrote (version 1, the manifest's own tree, the\\n-   publisher's origin), so a module whose markers were never retained\\n-   answers nil, never a recomputation passed off as the published\\n-   table.\\\"\\n+   from the referenced table address and the ORIGIN is the one the\\n+   record RECORDED at publication (`:yang.call-site/origin`, held to be\\n+   a well-formed occurrence origin), beside the record's version, the\\n+   manifest's own tree, and the referenced table. Nothing reconstructs\\n+   an origin from content: a replay of this retrieval answers the\\n+   recorded admission identity, and no other module's admission can be\\n+   confused with it.\\\"\\n   [handle address]\\n   (let [manifest (jing/get handle address nil)\\n         call-sites (:yin.module/call-sites manifest)]\\n@@ -374,15 +404,18 @@\\n                (map? call-sites) (jing/segment-address? (:table call-sites))\\n                (jing/segment-address? (:record call-sites)))\\n       (when-let [tree (stored-tree handle (:yin.module/tree manifest))]\\n-        (let [origin (publication-origin (:root tree))\\n-              stored-rows (jing/get handle (:table call-sites) nil)\\n+        (let [stored-rows (jing/get handle (:table call-sites) nil)\\n               stored-record (jing/get handle (:record call-sites) nil)\\n-              expected-record {:yang.call-site/version 1\\n-                               :yang.call-site/root (:yin.module/tree manifest)\\n-                               :yang.call-site/origin origin\\n-                               :yang.call-site/table (:table call-sites)}]\\n+              origin (:yang.call-site/origin stored-record)]\\n           (when (and (vector? stored-rows)\\n-                     (= expected-record stored-record))\\n+                     (= {:yang.call-site/version 1\\n+                         :yang.call-site/root (:yin.module/tree manifest)\\n+                         :yang.call-site/table (:table call-sites)}\\n+                        (select-keys stored-record\\n+                                     [:yang.call-site/version\\n+                                      :yang.call-site/root\\n+                                      :yang.call-site/table]))\\n+                     (vm/occurrence-origin? origin))\\n             {:origin origin, :root (:root tree), :rows stored-rows,\\n              :tree tree}))))))\\n \\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\nindex e62889fa..f3d28aa1 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\n@@ -1072,10 +1072,19 @@\\n    guard or invocation, run the transformer on the operand packets, validate\\n    and merge the result, finish the event, and reconsider the result at the\\n    same occurrence with depth + 1.\\\"\\n-  [st address entry {:keys [path rel frame shadow depth]}]\\n+  [st address entry {:keys [path rel frame shadow depth origin-of]}]\\n   (let [[st counter] (allocate-attempt st)\\n         ctx (:ctx st)\\n         macro-root (first (:yin.macro/tree entry))\\n+        ;; the occurrence provenance of the replaced application (§2.1):\\n+        ;; every output row whose content address is one of the input's\\n+        ;; own rows is the admitted syntax retained — a no-op expansion\\n+        ;; or an operand passed through — and keeps this origin; rows\\n+        ;; the expansion itself generated take the event's origin below\\n+        input-addrs (into #{address}\\n+                          (map first)\\n+                          (second (packet-of (:index st) address)))\\n+        admitted-origin (origin-of address)\\n         begin (fn [output error]\\n                 (event-row (attempt-id ctx counter)\\n                            (:source-batch st)\\n@@ -1124,7 +1133,17 @@\\n                                       :rel [],\\n                                       :frame {:parent (first ev), :root out},\\n                                       :shadow shadow,\\n-                                      :depth (inc depth)})))))))))))))\\n+                                      :depth (inc depth),\\n+                                      :origin-of\\n+                                      (fn [a]\\n+                                        (if (contains? input-addrs a)\\n+                                          admitted-origin\\n+                                          ;; the recorded expansion event\\n+                                          ;; row is the real expansion\\n+                                          ;; record (§8); its content\\n+                                          ;; address is the expansion\\n+                                          ;; origin (§2.1)\\n+                                          [:expansion (first ev)]))})))))))))))))\\n \\n \\n (defn- expand-children\\n@@ -1152,8 +1171,16 @@\\n (defn- expand-node\\n   \\\"§4.2. `loc` carries the absolute `path` in the batch's run tree, the\\n    `rel` path within the current `frame` (`{:root r :parent event|nil\\n-   :tree-index j}`), the lexical `shadow` set, and the expansion `depth`.\\n-   Returns `[st address']`; a failure sets `(:error st)`.\\\"\\n+   :tree-index j}`), the lexical `shadow` set, the expansion `depth`,\\n+   and `:origin-of` — the occurrence-provenance function of §2.1, which\\n+   answers the origin of the admission or generation event that\\n+   produced the syntax of the row at `address`. Every application\\n+   occurrence is recorded under its absolute path as it is visited, so\\n+   the finalizer can name each occurrence's real origin; an occurrence\\n+   replaced by an expansion is re-recorded by the expansion's own walk,\\n+   and a stale entry for a path the finished tree no longer applies at\\n+   is simply never read. Returns `[st address']`; a failure sets\\n+   `(:error st)`.\\\"\\n   [st address {:keys [shadow] :as loc}]\\n   (if (:error st)\\n     [st address]\\n@@ -1164,7 +1191,9 @@\\n         (expand-call st address (macro-of st (nth row 2) shadow) loc)\\n \\n         (= :application tag)\\n-        (let [op (nth row 2)\\n+        (let [st (update-in st [:call-site-origins]\\n+                            assoc (:path loc) ((:origin-of loc) address))\\n+              op (nth row 2)\\n               [st op'] (expand-node st op (-> loc\\n                                               (update :path conj 2)\\n                                               (update :rel conj 2)))]\\n@@ -1222,15 +1251,26 @@\\n    that actually executes. The frontend's own markers named the\\n    pre-expansion tree; expansion, tail marking and stand-in lowering\\n    recompute roots and paths, so no pre-expansion table can cover this\\n-   tree and the table is finalized here, over the finished rows. The\\n-   origin is the run member's admission origin — the same coordinate\\n-   the expander's event rows carry for a top-level frame. No\\n-   expansion-record address is fabricated for the batch as a whole:\\n-   the batch's attempts are individually recorded in the log, and the\\n-   final tree is their admitted input's finished form.\\\"\\n-  [row-set origin]\\n+   tree and the table is finalized here, over the finished rows.\\n+\\n+   Each row's origin is that occurrence's REAL origin (design §2.1,\\n+   §2.3): `occurrence-origins` maps the final occurrence path to the\\n+   origin of the admission or generation event that produced the\\n+   application syntax at that path — the admitted run member's source\\n+   origin for retained syntax (a no-op expansion keeps it valid), and\\n+   the `[:expansion record-address]` of the recorded expansion event\\n+   whose output introduced generated syntax. No expansion-record\\n+   address is fabricated: only event rows the expander actually\\n+   recorded appear. The table is therefore the explicit\\n+   final-root correspondence §2.3 requires — every generated occurrence\\n+   names its producing record, so an attribution consumer can reach the\\n+   event (its input root, call path, macro root, output root) from the\\n+   row alone.\\\"\\n+  [row-set occurrence-origins admission-origin]\\n   (vec (sort-by pr-str\\n-                (map (fn [[root path]] [origin root path :yang/call-site 1])\\n+                (map (fn [[root path]]\\n+                       [(get occurrence-origins path admission-origin)\\n+                        root path :yang/call-site 1])\\n                      (vm/application-occurrences row-set)))))\\n \\n \\n@@ -1245,9 +1285,15 @@\\n    actually executes — when, and only when, the selected member itself\\n    claimed C1 coverage (its own verdict in `batch-call-site-claims`); a\\n    sibling's claim never manufactures a table for it, and an unclaimed\\n-   batch carries no table at all, never an empty one. The table's\\n-   occurrence origin rides beside it as `: