{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"command -v -a git; cat .git/HEAD; rg -n --max-columns 240 --max-columns-preview 'slice9_site_tables|site-tables-file' --glob '\"'!*.log'\"' --glob '\"'!*.json'\"' --glob '\"'!*.edn'\"' --glob '\"'!*.md'\"' .; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794343000000-exception-track-surface-registry.md | sed -n '22,39p'; nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md | sed -n '10,27p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '22,34p;96,111p'\"","aggregated_output":"zsh:1: command not found: -v\nref: refs/heads/master\n./test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3247:      :site-tables-file \"slice9_site_tables.edn\"\n./test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3284:     (spit (str dir \"/slice9_site_tables.edn\")\n./test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3412:        (let [tables (edn/read-string (slurp (str dir \"/slice9_site_tables.edn\")))]\n./test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3418:              [\"slice9_evidence.edn\" \"slice9_unexercised.edn\" \"slice9_site_tables.edn\"])\n    22\t## Adjacent-surface acknowledgments (clojure seat reply 1794342913000 — accepted)\n    23\t\n    24\t- **cc2** (branch `cc2-transform-session-c1`, worktree datomworld-cc2): touches\n    25\t  `yang/safepoint.cljc`, `yin/vm/encoder.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc`, `yin/repl.cljc` —\n    26\t  NONE of these are on this registry; zero overlap with the clojure ceded surface. Acknowledged.\n    27\t- **cc3 commitment of record**: the clojure seat commits to lowering-provenance work on the\n    28\t  LINEARIZER side only (route (a): `semantic/linearize` + `derive`, no evaluator changes). Any\n    29\t  evaluator-runtime provenance work (route (b): `ast_walker`/`semantic`/`stack`/`register`) is\n    30\t  continuation-runtime surface and requires this seat's sign-off plus a coordination note BEFORE\n    31\t  dispatch; impossibility on route (a) escalates to the owner with both seats' evidence.\n    32\t- **(B)-fallback pre-claim obligation**: if R4's proof fails and the fallback touches\n    33\t  `encoder.cljc`, `publish.cljc`, `linearize.cljc`, or `derive.cljc`, THIS seat must claim those\n    34\t  files here before the fallback round starts; the clojure seat yields immediately.\n    35\t  Pre-claimed as a conditional: none needed yet — the fallback scope is clojure.cljc-internal\n    36\t  until its round brief is written.\n    37\t\n    38\tRule: a round from any other track that would edit one of these files' listed regions queues\n    39\tbehind the owning round (one-writer, per file+region). Everything else in every frontend\n    10\t\n    11\t## Rulings derived (U1–U3)\n    12\t\n    13\t**U1 — an occurrence is not its content address.** The occurrence identity is **(content address, origin)** — the address as reached through a specific transformation event. \"Two occurrences of identical content\" is well-formed: they differ in history, not content. The per-occurrence origins in the call-site side table, keyed on `[origin root-address path]`, ARE the query that recovers the distinction (design §2.1/§2.3). Codex's P1-a objection (\"identical generated/input applications share an address, so provenance cannot be classified by content\") is ANSWERED: provenance is not classified by content — it is read from the retained history: the expansion-event record behind a `[:expansion record-address]` origin, the source position behind a `[:source …]` origin. Never minted, never guessed from content.\n    14\t\n    15\t**U2 — admission identity is history-recoverable, declared by the composition.** If the composition's history contains two admission events, bijectivity demands both are recoverable — collapsing them into one deterministic token loses information and violates unitarity. If the history contains one event, minting a fresh token fabricates history — the violation in the other direction. Only the composition knows which its history contains, so the composition DECLARES the distinction when it exists (the caller-owned admission scope / explicit `:yin.link.publish/admission` token), and the deterministic content-derived default is CORRECT precisely because re-publication of identical content with no declared distinction IS one event. Codex's P1-b objection is ANSWERED on these grounds: the requirement is not \"every admission mints a fresh token\" (that fabricates history) but \"every DISTINCT event is representable and recoverable\" (the scoped/explicit mechanism provides it; the composition supplies it when its history contains two events).\n    16\t\n    17\t**U3 — the general principle.** Identity is never minted from structure OR from process. Occurrence distinctness is either (i) READ from retained history (per-occurrence origins, expansion events), or (ii) DECLARED by the party whose history it is (composition-supplied admission identities). Provenance remains recoverable by address or query over datoms — axiom 2 holds in both channels.\n    18\t\n    19\t## Consequences\n    20\t\n    21\t- The cc1 branch state (d91489f7) STANDS as the implementation of U1/U2 — no further design-level code change is required to answer the codex reject; the objection is resolved on the record by this ruling.\n    22\t- The landing sequence proceeds: astra re-check against this ruling → codex re-gate → merge to master → the exception track's R3 dispatch → adoption rounds 2–4 + D1.\n    23\t- Addendum A's occurrence contract is unaffected in shape: K = [origin root-address path], `:yang/call-site 1`, the occurrence/content boundary — all unchanged. U1 clarifies WHAT an origin identifies (an event in the retained history), not how it attaches.\n    24\t\n    25\t— drafted by the yang.clojure orchestrator seat from the owner's ruling in session\n    26\t\n    27\t## Sign-off (owner, 2026-11-09 01:5x +07)\n    22\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    25\t\n    26\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    27\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    28\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    29\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    30\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    31\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    32\t- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n    33\t\n    34\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    96\t- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n    97\t  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n    98\t\n    99\t**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n   100\tcanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n   101\tshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n   102\t`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n   103\tquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n   104\tconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n   105\tretired `:global` tag in\n   106\t[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n   107\tcase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n   108\t\n   109\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   110\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   111\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
