ed`,\n    23\t  `:dynamic-callee`), enclosing definition, and self-delegation mark —\n    24\t  recorded in the versioned [`slice9_site_tables.edn`](slice9_site_tables.edn)\n    25\t  (`:version 1`) with the per-fixture canonical-source-hash of the inlined\n    26\t  adaptation (R2b case 3's referent: a trace read against a differing hash\n    27\t  refuses).\n    28\t- **The query.** An upstream function-arity is credited when both C lanes\n    29\t  record (a) the arity-preserving guest definition's body entry at the same\n    30\t  arity through any real site (the degenerate 1:1 path), or (b) a body\n    31\t  entry of a derived correspondence carrier — the guest/context callable\n    32\t  and arity the derived correspondence names (`:attribution\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-10 20:51:12 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 03:51:12 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nQ1 VERDICT: Approve route (a) for bounded cc3 acceptance, with native-image PC→K reconstruction IN cc3. It does not establish general evaluator-runtime provenance.\n\nThe necessary static information is available without evaluator changes:\n\n- `project-rows` actually returns `[pc origin root path]`, accepting one optional origin; publication currently discards that provenance. cc3 must resolve each application’s origin from the validated occurrence table, rather than assign one origin indiscriminately across mixed source/expansion occurrences. See [linearize.cljc:188](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc:188) and [publish.cljc:310](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:310).\n- Stack lowering has each source semantic PC available while emitting a different instruction sequence. Register lowering preserves layout. Therefore native-call-PC→semantic-PC→K reconstruction belongs in `derive`, within route (a); it is neither a necessary deferral nor evaluator-runtime work. See [derive.cljc:74](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc:74), [derive.cljc:154](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc:154), and [design §2.4:75](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:75).\n\nThree acceptance conditions must be explicit:\n\n1. **Separate complete C1 coverage from oracle coverage.** The oracle stamps selected guest-call shapes and deliberately excludes dispatcher/library applications; its site table cannot equal the complete C1 application set. Establish the verified bijection between oracle sites and their selected compiled occurrences, then independently verify all C1 occurrences and all claimed instruction mappings, including uncredited helpers. Preserve duplicate-content occurrences, repeated executions, and transformation multiplicities. Rule-R applications receive C1 markers but lower to `:define`, not fictitious calls. Evidence: [oracle stamping:2192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2192), [design coverage:43](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:43), [independent verification:225](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:225).\n\n2. **Bind the final instrumented tree using retained history.** Contrary to the scoping’s chronology, current `kernel-compile` compiles first, then stamps by inserting applications. Final roots/paths and C1 coverage must therefore account for that transformation. Both C lanes already receive the same compiled value. A fixture identifier may supply a recorded admission coordinate; `[:oracle/fixture fixture-id]` alone is not a valid persisted origin. Evidence: [kernel-compile:2449](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2449), [kernel-row:2858](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2858), [origin validator:938](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:938), [owner ruling U1–U3:13](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md:13).\n\n3. **Do not confuse static correspondence with runtime correctness.** The oracle uses a shared “latest site” guest cell, read at body entry. A static bijection cannot prove preservation across interleaving, suspension, migration, or origin-sensitive loading. cc3 must independently verify its claimed oracle dispatch/body-entry correspondence in both lanes and refuse ambiguous or unsupported cases. General continuation-safe caller context remains a named continuation-seat boundary item. Evidence: [site channel:1983](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1983), [design runtime requirement:101](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:101), [suspension gate:354](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:354).\n\nThus, emission plus a producer-generated bijection alone is **insufficient**. The minimal boundary-respecting remedy is an independent harness-side reconstruction/check, complete static coverage checks, and explicit runtime claim limits. If required oracle fixtures cannot satisfy those limits, cc3 remains incomplete and the seats must coordinate before evaluator work, as [the registry requires:27](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/exception-track/1794343000000-exception-track-surface-registry.md:27). Successful reconstruction and runtime coverage are **unverified** here.\n\nQ2 VERDICT: Choose (ii), a separate `slice9_correspondence.edn` companion referencing unchanged v1 evidence.\n\n**The design’s schema takes precedence over the scoping’s incompatible sketch.** D1 is a separately addressed, closed-schema envelope. Its `:evidence` preserves OracleEvidence verbatim; `:origin-bindings` relates literal evidence-local IDs to K. The design expressly prohibits copying evidence rows into another durable site-table shape and says D1 does not revise old tables. Therefore “every site keyed by K” should be a verified query/view through the binding relation, not a second persisted attribution table. See [design §4.1:180](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:180), [verbatim requirement:202](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:202), and [historical compatibility:237](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:237).\n\nThe reader search found:\n\n- The only explicit executable file read of `slice9_site_tables.edn` is the temporary-output round-trip check at [latte_oracle_test.clj:3412](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3412).\n- Attribution consumes integer-keyed **in-memory** tables through lookups such as `(get table site)`, at [2340](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2340) and [2361](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2361). Evidence records the companion filename at [3247](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3247).\n- Contrary to the scoping, `src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj` currently generates manifest/throw/case inventories, not slice9 site tables: [generator:52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj:52). External readers are **unverified**.\n\nThe existing test is not compatibility assurance: it starts from evidence whose inline site tables were removed, then checks only version and fixture count. Nil table payloads can satisfy those assertions. See [writer:3299](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3299) and [round-trip:3388](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3388).\n\nThe companion avoids mixed-key lookup failures, competing classifications, and silently changed history under `:version 1`. Pin the exact evidence files/commit, schema, source-hash algorithm, derivation and validator; retain traces and reconstruction inputs by address. Scope bindings by fixture/compilation because integer IDs restart per compilation. Historical runs lacking retained admissions cannot acquire invented origins: produce a new recorded run and preserve the old evidence. This follows [design:219](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:219).\n\nQ3 VERDICT: Amend the acceptance sequence to cc3 → D1 independent verification → cc4 final four-host/budget acceptance.\n\nD1 requires cc2/cc3’s retained inputs, occurrence relations, bindings and artifact mappings. Its verification procedure does **not** depend on passing cc4 first. Conversely, acceptance of a frontend relying on D1 must cite the completed independent verification result. The distinction is explicit in [design §5:243](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:243) and [§8 steps 3–5:373](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:373).\n\ncc4 preparation, static equality checks and baseline measurements may proceed earlier. A separately labelled static-C1 acceptance need not consume D1, but cannot count as durable-correspondence acceptance. Full cc4 budgeting also includes D1 and retained reconstruction inputs, so final measurements should cover the actual artifacts: [measurement scope:294](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:294). Numeric preflight bounds must precede tagged cc3/D1 runs; they cannot wait until cc4.\n\nOwner risks: the scoping additionally requires empty stale-site diagnostics for passing fixtures, whereas the current oracle explicitly records nonempty diagnostics on passing fixtures ([3248](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3248)). Preserve and independently reproduce those diagnostics rather than silently strengthen the historical oracle contract. General runtime provenance remains an explicit dependency; oracle stamping alone does not close production adoption.\n\nRead-only inspection against working-tree master HEAD `498cba4c09ef46aa4ec5a0763e2405bb89089fb6`. No files edited or tests run. cc2 landing is assumed as instructed; implementation acceptance, measured budgets and cross-host results remain **unverified**."}}
